# Editor / OS noise
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store

# Backups made while editing configs
*.bak
*.orig
*.rej

# Anything a tool generates into the config dir at runtime.
# Hyprland itself writes nothing here, but hyprpm (plugin manager) does.
hypr/hyprpm/
hypr/.hyprpm*

# Generated by bin/theme.sh, not authored. ~/.config/kitty is a symlink into
# this checkout, so the theme file kitty includes lands in the repo whether we
# like it or not - and it is derived state that changes on every theme switch,
# which would make each switch show up as a diff. The themes it is generated
# FROM (themes/*.conf) are tracked; this is not.
kitty/theme.conf

# Generated by bin/theme.sh, like kitty/theme.conf above and for the same
# reason: ~/.config/hypr is a symlink into this checkout, so the colour file
# hyprlock sources lands in the repo. The theme files it is generated FROM are
# tracked; this is not.
hyprlock-colors.conf

# Scales chosen in the Display page of quickshell's settings. ~/.config/hypr is
# a symlink into this checkout, so a file Hyprland requires by name lands in
# the repo whether we like it or not - the same situation as kitty's generated
# theme above. Per machine on purpose: the Framework's panel is 204 ppi and the
# desktop's monitor is not, so a value committed here would be wrong somewhere.
hypr/monitors_local.lua

# Per-machine tmux overrides, not authored here. See the bottom of
# tmux/tmux.conf: ~/.config/tmux is a symlink into this checkout, so a file
# tmux loads by name lands in the repo. The Framework uses one to square off
# the status bar pills that kitty draws rounded.
tmux/tmux_local.conf

# Python leaves these beside any script it imports.
__pycache__/

# WALLPAPERS YOU ADD YOURSELF STAY ON THE MACHINE. This repository is public,
# so a file committed here is redistributed to anyone who clones it - fine for
# the set that came with it (wallpapers/README.md says where those are from,
# and they are already tracked, which an ignore rule does not undo), wrong for
# a photograph of your own.
#
# So new images in wallpapers/ are ignored and simply never offered for
# commit. The picker reads the DIRECTORY, not git, so an ignored wallpaper
# works exactly like a tracked one - it is only absent from a fresh clone and
# from the other machine.
#
# To commit one deliberately - your own work, or something CC0:
#     git add -f wallpapers/whatever.webp
wallpapers/*.png
wallpapers/*.jpg
wallpapers/*.jpeg
wallpapers/*.webp
